{"compatibility":"as-is","safety_note_md":"# SafeStan Safety Note

Compatibility: as-is.

Protected data: `y`.

Generated files: `model_safe.stan`, `data_safe.json`.

No changes were required. The protected observation `y` is a top-level data variable and is observed exactly once with a plain built-in sampling statement after both parameters have received built-in priors. The other data variables, `N` and `m`, are context inputs used for dimensions and deterministic matrix construction only.
","model_safe_stan":"data {
  int<lower = 1> N; // Number of point masses
  array[N - 1] real<lower = 0.0> y; // Data
  real m; // Mass of particles
}
transformed data {
  // This matrix is constant. We build it once
  matrix[N, N] K_unscaled = rep_matrix(0, N, N);

  for(n in 1:N) {
    if(n == 1) {
      K_unscaled[n, n] = 1.0 / m;
      K_unscaled[n, n + 1] = -1.0 / m;
    } else if(n == N) {
      K_unscaled[n, n - 1] = -1.0 / m;
      K_unscaled[n, n] = 1.0 / m;
    } else {
      K_unscaled[n, n - 1] = -1.0 / m;
      K_unscaled[n, n] = 2.0 / m;
      K_unscaled[n, n + 1] = -1.0 / m;
    }
  }
}
parameters {
  real<lower = 0.0> k;
  real<lower = 0.0> sigma;
}
transformed parameters {
  vector[N - 1] eigs;

  {
    matrix[N, N] K = k * K_unscaled;

    eigs = eigenvalues_sym(K)[2:N];
  }
}
model {
  k ~ normal(1.0, 1.0);
  sigma ~ normal(0.0, 1.0);

  // Don't forget, the resonance frequencies are the square roots of the
  // eigenvalues
  y ~ normal(sqrt(eigs), sigma);
}
","data_safe_json":"{"N":2,"m":3.9659118167395517,"y":[2.0]}","protected_variables":["y"]}
